title: Human Risk in Cloud Security
page: human-risk.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: The most common way into a cloud tenant is that nobody broke anything, so measure what people do rather than reduce them to a job title.

== Nobody broke anything
layout: text
section: top
Almost every control on this site assumes an attacker must break something. The most common way into a cloud tenant is that nobody did: someone approved a prompt, reset a password for a caller, or paid a quietly edited invoice.
notes:
Almost every control on the rest of this site assumes an attacker has to break something. This deck is about the other case. The most common way into a cloud tenant is that nobody broke anything: someone approved a prompt, reset a password for a caller, or paid an invoice that had been quietly edited.

The session that prompted the page put it as a question about abstraction. If you reduce an employee to one variable, usually job title, you get a model that is convenient and wrong in ways you cannot see.

The deck covers what the behaviour data showed, how the attacks arrive, why cloud makes them worse, how to detect them with signals you already have, how to build a program, and how to protect your own accounts.

== Why this belongs on a cloud security site
layout: text
section: why-here
In cloud, the gap between an attacker persuading someone and having your data is unusually short. A valid session token won by social engineering looks, to every downstream control, exactly like the legitimate user.
notes:
In cloud there is no network boundary doing quiet second-line work. A valid session token obtained by social engineering looks, to every control downstream, exactly like the legitimate user it was taken from: right identity, plausible device, normal working hours. The controls this site spends most of its time on assume a technical failure to detect. Here there is none.

The other reason is that the Friday sessions kept returning to it. Five of the twelve recaps in the summer of 2026 were substantially about the human layer. The technical half of that material already lives on the site, and the behavioural half had nowhere to go.

== Role is a first draft, not a finding
layout: text
section: meeple
Security programs routinely reduce an employee to one variable, usually job title or seniority. Role is a reasonable starting hypothesis when designing access controls. The problem is that the first draft almost always becomes the final one.
notes:
At the 2026-08-28 session, Stryker, then Director of Threat Analysis at Fable Security, presented a talk called "The Meeple Problem." A meeple is the single-colour wooden playing piece from board games like Carcassonne: one shape, one move, one thing it can ever do. That abstraction is right for a board game and wrong for a person, yet security programs routinely reduce an employee to one variable, usually job title or seniority, and then treat that first draft as final.

Her argument was not that role is useless. Role is a reasonable starting hypothesis when designing access controls, and assumptions built on experience are valuable. The problem is that the first draft almost always becomes the final one, and acting on an untested hypothesis is a different thing from acting on a measurement.

== Why the role shortcut persists
layout: compare
section: meeple
col: Organisational logic | In principle, access follows function and controls follow access | Coherent right up until you check who has access
col: Regulatory framing | NIST, GDPR and NYDFS describe role-based controls and training | Programs written to satisfy them install role as the primary variable
col: History | A list of people and job titles was nearly the only data | Better telemetry exists now
Stryker gave three reasons the shortcut persists.
notes:
She gave three reasons. The first is organisational logic: in principle access follows function and controls follow access, so a role-level generalisation looks defensible. "All developers handle production data, therefore all developers need the sensitive-data reminder" is coherent right up until you check, and find they do not all have that access.

The second is regulatory framing. NIST, GDPR, NYDFS and the state regimes in New York, Texas and California all describe role-based controls and role-based training, which quietly installs role as the primary variable in any program written to satisfy them.

The third is historical. For much of the field's short life, a list of people and their job titles was close to the only data available. That made role a sensible starting point then, and better telemetry exists now.

== Where role and seniority fail to predict behaviour
layout: table
section: what-data-says
row: Variable | What the data showed
row: Seniority | No correlation at all with who clicked a phishing link
row: Tenure | The newest hires failed phishing simulations most
row: Role buckets | Internal spread so wide the average concealed most of the signal
row: AI-enabled employees | Technical controls held better, volitional ones worse
Stryker drew these from her AI Behavior Index, an older executive survey and her in-house phishing analysis.
notes:
Drawing on the first volume of her AI Behavior Index at Fable Security, an older executive survey, and her own in-house phishing analysis, Stryker showed several places where role and seniority fail to predict what people do.

Seniority showed no correlation at all with who clicked a phishing link. That is worth sitting with, because a great deal of program design assumes the opposite in both directions, either that executives are the risk or that they are the exception. Tenure did show a signal, pointing the unintuitive way: the newest hires failed phishing simulations most, which is actionable in a way an average is not.

Role buckets varied so widely internally that the reported average concealed most of the signal. A department mean can be produced by a uniform population or by a small high-risk group inside an otherwise careful one, and the two need completely different responses. AI-enabled employees were more likely to have the configuration right and more likely to make a judgement call that bypassed it.

== The index preview, as she presented it
layout: stats
section: what-data-says
stat: 32 | Environments analysed | Covering 270,000 employees
stat: 34% | Detected using AI at work | Measured use
stat: ~50% | Tell surveys they use AI | Self-reported use
Reported as she presented them, not independently checked. She explained the gap as AI features switched on inside already-approved software, and said she distrusted both numbers.
notes:
She previewed the index a week before the talk, at the 2026-08-21 session, as an analysis of behaviour patterns across 270,000 employees and 32 environments. Two findings from that preview belong beside the four on the previous slide, reported as she presented them rather than as independently checked results.

Measured AI use ran well below self-reported use: 34% of employees were detected using AI at work, against the roughly 50% who tell surveys they use it. At the 2026-08-28 talk she explained the gap as AI features switched on inside software the company had already approved, and said she distrusted both numbers.

Legal staff stood out. Legal professionals showed particularly high rates of risky behaviour, which she attributed to jobs that run on heavy email and document handling. It is a role-level result, but the explanation offered was about what the work involves, not the title.

== What the sample size does and does not settle
layout: compare
section: what-data-says
col: What size helps with | Small-sample noise unlikely to explain a population-wide figure like 34% | But cohorts shrink as variables are added; she reported none under 100
col: What size does not answer | Unsafe browsing meant whatever each tool flagged | AI use measured by a file-upload proxy | She called them correlation, not causation | Representativeness: the environments were one vendor's customers
The seniority and tenure findings came from her in-house phishing analysis, which the large figure does not vouch for.
notes:
The group pushed back throughout on what the data could and could not support, which was largely the point of the talk. The failure mode being described is treating a first-pass grouping as a settled fact, and a room accepting one speaker's numbers uncritically would have reproduced it.

The sample size helps with that weighing, but only for part of the list. The AI findings, the role buckets and the legal result come from the index, which covered roughly 270,000 to 290,000 people across 32 customer environments; the two sessions gave slightly different totals. At that size, small-sample noise is an unlikely explanation for a population-wide figure like 34%, though cohorts shrink as variables are added and she did not report any under 100.

Size does not answer the questions the room raised, which were about definitions and causes. Unsafe browsing meant whatever each customer's endpoint tooling was tuned to flag. She could not detect AI use directly and used AI file-upload rates as a proxy. She called her behavioural findings correlation rather than causation. Nor does size make the sample representative: the environments were one vendor's customers.

The seniority and tenure findings did not come from the index at all. They came from her own in-house phishing analysis, for which the recaps give no population size.

== Four ways in, one prize
layout: figure
section: how-attacks-arrive
figure: human-attack-shapes
Four shapes account for most of what the sessions discussed. The usual controls do not defend them equally well.
notes:
Four shapes account for most of what the sessions discussed: phishing, business email compromise, rented phishing kits, and a help-desk MFA reset. They are not equally well defended by the usual controls.

Each one ends in a session that downstream controls treat as the real person. As the caption puts it, all four end at the same place, a valid session, and downstream controls see the legitimate user. The next four slides take the shapes one at a time.

== Phishing: the old advice expired
layout: text
section: how-attacks-arrive
Advice about bad grammar and generic greetings trained people to detect production quality rather than intent, and production quality got cheap. What survives is verifying the request through a channel the message did not choose.
notes:
Advice built around spotting bad grammar, generic greetings and odd formatting was training people to detect production quality rather than intent, and production quality is exactly what got cheap.

At the 2026-07-10 session Daniel walked the group through the stages of an AI-assisted campaign. Models scan for viable targets and tailor each message to its recipient, which makes a mass campaign behave like spear phishing. Autonomous follow-up replies are technically possible and were described as still emerging rather than routine.

What survives is verification of the request itself, through a channel the message did not choose.

== Business email compromise
layout: text
section: how-attacks-arrive
BEC gives technical controls the least to work with: no link to detonate, no attachment to scan, often a compromised mailbox in a real thread. Verify payment-detail changes out of band, on a number you already had.
notes:
BEC is the case where technical controls have least to work with. The message carries no link to detonate and no attachment to scan, and it often arrives from a compromised mailbox, inserted into a real conversation about a real invoice.

At the 2026-07-31 session the group worked through a small law firm's exposure and agreed that technical controls alone cannot prevent these attacks and that strong processes and user training are essential.

The controls that do work are procedural: verify payment-detail changes out of band, on a number you already had.

== Phishing kits rented by the month
layout: text
section: how-attacks-arrive
One phishing-as-a-service offering automates Microsoft 365 account compromise for roughly $250 a month, including scanning captured mailboxes for wire-transfer opportunities. That rents out an end-to-end BEC pipeline and collapses the skill required.
notes:
At the 2026-06-19 session Thomas raised a phishing-as-a-service offering that automates Microsoft 365 account compromise for roughly $250 a month. It includes automatically scanning captured mailboxes for wire-transfer opportunities and sending contextual replies to victims.

That is an end-to-end BEC pipeline rented by the month, and it collapses the skill required to run one.

The group noted a partial consolation: rented infrastructure is reused across many targets, so the resulting volume can make campaigns easier to detect than a bespoke one.

== A help-desk reset bypasses your strongest factor
layout: text
section: how-attacks-arrive
An attacker who calls the help desk, impersonates an employee and obtains an MFA reset never has to defeat the existing factor. The defence is a defined proof-of-identity step, applied without exception.
notes:
Voice-based social engineering against IT support is the path that bypasses the strongest authentication you own. An attacker who calls the help desk, impersonates an employee and obtains an MFA reset never has to defeat the existing factor. Phishing-resistant authentication does not help, because the attack does not touch the old credential at all.

The defence is a process control at the help desk: a defined proof-of-identity step applied without exception. That is organisationally harder than deploying hardware keys and considerably cheaper.

== Why the cloud consequences are worse
layout: compare
section: cloud-specific
col: The session is the prize | A stolen token replays without further authentication | Often past MFA, and for longer than anyone intends
col: Mailbox access is lateral movement | A password-reset channel for every SaaS app the person uses | A trusted channel for the next phish | A searchable archive of finance conversations
col: The blast radius is an API call away | One over-permissioned identity plus a valid session | Can reach production data without touching a monitored network
Three properties make a cloud compromise worse than the equivalent on-premises one.
notes:
Three properties make the cloud consequences worse than the equivalent on-premises compromise.

The session is the prize. Token theft after a successful phish gives an attacker something that replays without any further authentication, often past MFA entirely, and often for longer than anyone intends because refresh lifetimes are rarely audited.

Mailbox access is lateral movement. A compromised mailbox in a cloud productivity suite is also a password-reset channel for every SaaS application the person uses, an internal-trust channel for the next phish, and a searchable archive of exactly the finance conversations BEC needs.

The blast radius is an API call away. On-premises, an attacker with a user's credentials still has to move. In cloud, a single over-permissioned identity plus a valid session can reach production data without touching a network the defender monitors.

== Hunt with signals you already have
layout: table
section: detection
row: Signal | What to know
row: Non-interactive sign-ins | Token replay; the interactive log looks clean
row: Mailbox rules and forwarding | The highest-signal BEC indicator
row: Impossible travel, unfamiliar devices | Inputs to a hunt, not alerts to close
row: Help desk credential resets | Matched to later sign-ins, they show vishing
row: EDR unsafe browsing | Most EDR emits it; few teams route it usefully
row: AI-use measurement | A gateway, a CASB or SaaS app discovery
Stryker's closing advice: pair unsafe browsing with AI use. Cohorts built from them will not match your org chart.
notes:
The useful reframing from the 2026-08-28 session was to treat this as threat hunting applied to people rather than as an awareness problem. Most of the inputs already exist in a normal cloud estate.

Non-interactive sign-in logs are the gap that matters for token replay, and easy to overlook because the interactive log looks clean. Mailbox rule creation and forwarding changes are the single highest-signal BEC indicator: an attacker who has taken a mailbox almost always hides the evidence from its owner first. Impossible travel and unfamiliar-device sign-ins are inputs to a hunt rather than alerts to close. Help desk credential-reset events, correlated against subsequent sign-in location and device, are where the vishing path becomes visible, and almost nobody reviews them. Most EDR products already emit unsafe-browsing telemetry, and few teams route it anywhere useful. The last input is whatever measures AI use in your environment.

Stryker's closing recommendation was specifically to pair the EDR unsafe-browsing signal with the AI-usage signal, on the grounds that together they describe volitional behaviour in a way neither does alone. Cohorts built from those signals will not match your org chart, which is the point.

== Building the program, in order
layout: checklist
section: program
check: Measure before you group: a quarter of signals, no action
check: Build cohorts from behaviour, then check them against role
check: Fix process controls first: payment verification, help desk proofing
check: Target interventions: new hires' first ninety days, heavy AI users
check: Re-measure, and let cohorts dissolve
A workable progression, ordered so each step produces something the next one needs.
notes:
A workable progression, ordered so that each step produces something the next one needs.

Measure before you group: collect the behavioural signals for a quarter without acting on them. The purpose is to find out whether your role buckets have any internal consistency, which is a question with a real answer and usually a surprising one. Then build cohorts from behaviour and check them against role. Where they agree, role was a fine proxy and you have saved effort. Where they diverge, you have found the population your previous program was missing.

Fix the process controls first. Out-of-band verification for payment changes and identity proofing at the help desk stop entire attack classes and need no behavioural data at all. Doing them first means the measurement work is not load-bearing while it matures.

Then target the interventions. New hires in their first ninety days is a defensible cohort on the tenure finding alone. Heavy AI users are a different cohort needing different guidance, not more of the same.

Finally, re-measure and let cohorts dissolve. A person who has moved out of a high-risk pattern should stop being treated as high-risk, or you have rebuilt the meeple with more steps.

== Physical security and the home office
layout: text
section: physical
Tailgating, badging and laptop locks predate cloud and did not go away, and the home office is now permanent for a large share of staff. Security awareness has to be teachable and livable.
notes:
The 2026-06-26 session covered the part that predates cloud and did not go away: tailgating, badging, laptop locks, and how to extend any of it to home offices now that the home office is permanent for a large share of staff.

Drawing partly on Stryker's time in the Marine Corps, the discussion landed on a constraint worth keeping in view when writing policy of any kind: security awareness has to be teachable and livable. A home-office policy nobody can follow produces no security and a measurable amount of contempt for the next policy you write.

The same session traded notes on password managers, including the practical difficulty of moving a 75-year-old family member from one to another. It is a small example of a general point, which is that usability is part of the threat model rather than a concession made against it.

== Your own accounts sit on the same attack path
layout: text
section: personal-credentials
In the LastPass breach, a keylogger on an engineer's personal home computer captured the master password to the engineer's corporate vault. The cloud credentials inside it were used to steal customer vault backups.
notes:
Password managers came up at the 2026-06-26, 2026-08-28 and 2026-09-04 sessions, and personal accounts belong here because they sit on the same attack path as work ones.

In the LastPass breach, a keylogger on an engineer's personal home computer captured the master password to the engineer's LastPass corporate vault, and the cloud credentials inside it were used to steal customer vault backups.

The next slides give the short version of the advice for your own accounts.

== Credential hygiene for your own accounts
layout: checklist
section: personal-credentials
check: Let a password manager generate every password
check: Prefer passkeys or security keys; treat SMS as the fallback
check: Keep recovery codes where you can still reach them
check: Sign up to hear when your details leak
check: Freeze your credit when identity documents leak
check: Set up access for the people who will need it
notes:
The page gives the source behind each of these points.

CISA's advice is to create long, random, unique passwords with a password manager, and it counts the free managers built into web browsers among the options. Uniqueness is what stops one breach from spreading: a reused password lets an attacker replay it against every other service that shares it, which is credential stuffing.

Keep the recovery path as strong as the sign-in. Save the backup or recovery codes each account offers somewhere you can still reach if you lose that account or your phone. Google suggests a printed copy kept with your passport or other important documents, and says never to share them. The help-desk attack works against personal accounts too: whoever can reset your second factor never has to defeat it.

Have I Been Pwned emails you for free when an address you register appears in a new data breach, and its Pwned Passwords check sends only the first five characters of a password's hash. Several password managers run a similar check over what you store, including 1Password Watchtower, Apple Passwords, Bitwarden's Data Breach report (free for all users), Google's compromised-password alerts, and Keeper BreachWatch (a paid add-on).

== MFA methods, as CISA rates them
layout: table
section: personal-credentials
row: Method | CISA's view
row: FIDO/WebAuthn, the standard behind passkeys | The only widely available phishing-resistant form
row: Authenticator-app codes | Vulnerable to phishing
row: SMS and voice codes | A last resort, also exposed to SIM swap attacks
CISA calls phishing-resistant MFA the gold standard, and says any MFA is better than none.
notes:
Prefer passkeys or security keys for MFA, and treat SMS as the fallback.

CISA's fact sheet on phishing-resistant MFA says any MFA is better than none and calls phishing-resistant MFA the gold standard. CISA's MFA guidance names FIDO/WebAuthn, the standard behind passkeys, as the only widely available form.

The same fact sheet rates authenticator-app codes as vulnerable to phishing, and describes SMS and voice codes as a last resort that is also exposed to SIM swap attacks.

== When identity documents leak
layout: compare
section: personal-credentials
col: Credit freeze | Free to place and lift in the US | Done with each of the three major bureaus | Blocks new credit in your name, including your own applications
col: Fraud alert | The lighter option | Lenders must verify your identity before granting credit | Your report stays visible to them
A password manager does nothing for leaked driver's licence or other government-issued ID numbers.
notes:
In September 2026 identity-verification company IDScan.net said in its breach notice that names and driver's licence or other government-issued ID numbers may have been accessed, and the 2026-09-04 session spent time on what stolen licence data enables. A password manager does nothing for that kind of exposure.

In the US, placing and lifting a credit freeze is free but has to be done with each of the three major credit bureaus. The FTC notes that a freeze blocks new credit in your name, including credit you apply for yourself, until you lift it.

A fraud alert is the lighter option: lenders must verify your identity before granting credit, but your report stays visible to them.

== Plan for the day you cannot open your vault
layout: table
section: personal-credentials
row: Mechanism | What it allows
row: 1Password | A family organizer can recover a member's account
row: Apple Legacy Contact | Certain account data after your death, excluding iCloud Keychain passwords and passkeys
row: Bitwarden Emergency Access | A trusted contact can request your vault; a premium feature
row: Google Inactive Account Manager | Shares chosen data after a period of inactivity
row: Keeper Emergency Access | Up to five contacts on consumer accounts
Check what the one you rely on covers before anyone needs it.
notes:
The usability point cuts both ways: a vault nobody else can open becomes a family's problem on the day you cannot open it yourself, and the mechanisms differ in what they hand over.

1Password lets a family organizer recover a member's account. Apple's Legacy Contact gives someone access to certain account data after your death, but excludes the passwords and passkeys stored in iCloud Keychain. Bitwarden Emergency Access, a premium feature, lets a trusted contact request your vault, granted when you approve it or after a wait time expires. Google's Inactive Account Manager shares chosen data after a period of inactivity, and Keeper Emergency Access allows up to five contacts on consumer accounts.

Check what the one you rely on covers before anyone needs it.

== Pitfalls
layout: list
section: pitfalls
item: Treating training completion rate as the metric
item: Publishing click rates by department
item: Punitive framing that raises the cost of a report
item: Assuming executives are the target and juniors are not
item: Buying a tool before you have a question
The most valuable behaviour in this domain is a fast report from someone who has just realised they clicked something.
notes:
The first pitfall is treating the annual training completion rate as the metric. It measures whether people clicked through a course, which is not correlated with whether they will click through a phish. Publishing click rates by department produces exactly the average that conceals the signal, and it teaches people to under-report rather than to report.

On punitive framing: the most valuable behaviour in this entire domain is a fast report from someone who has just realised they clicked something. Anything that raises the cost of that report is a net loss, however satisfying it feels.

On assuming executives are the target and juniors are not: whaling is real, and it is not what the click data says about who falls for things. On buying a tool before you have a question: most of the signals in this deck come from products you already run, so the gap is usually routing and correlation, not acquisition.

== Is security awareness training the same as human risk management?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider how a traditional program groups people, and what each group receives.
notes:
This is a question from the page's quick answers. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== Training is one intervention
layout: answer
section: faq
Human risk management measures which people are at risk and chooses interventions from that measurement. It groups people by observed behaviour rather than by role or department.
notes:
No. Awareness training is one intervention; human risk management is the practice of measuring which people are at risk and choosing interventions from that measurement.

The difference shows up in how you group people. Traditional programs group by role or department and deliver the same annual course to everyone in the bucket. A human risk program groups by observed behaviour: who clicked, who uses unsanctioned AI tools, who joined three weeks ago. Training is still in the toolkit, but it stops being the whole toolkit.

== Is the help desk really the weak point?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider which controls a phone call to IT support can get around.
notes:
This is the second question from the page's quick answers. Again, ask it before showing the answer.

== Often the cheapest path in
layout: answer
section: faq
An MFA reset obtained by impersonation bypasses even phishing-resistant authentication. The defence is a process: a proof-of-identity step, a callback to a known number, or a manager's approval, applied without exception.
notes:
It is frequently the cheapest path in, because it can bypass controls the endpoint cannot. An attacker who calls IT support, impersonates an employee and obtains an MFA reset never has to defeat the existing factor, which means even phishing-resistant authentication does not help.

The defence is identity verification at the help desk itself, and it is a process control rather than a product: a defined proof-of-identity step, a callback to a known number, or approval from a manager, applied without exception for reset requests.
